import TruthCore.Greek.Aristotle.Physics.BookII.Chapter5

namespace TruthCore.Greek.Aristotle.Physics

/--
Opening distinction in Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197a36-197b1: spontaneity has wider scope
than chance; everything from chance is spontaneous, but not everything spontaneous is from
chance.

Source: Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197a36-197b1 (Bekker pagination).
CTS work: `urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0086.tlg031`.
-/
def SpontaneityBroaderThanChanceReading
    (EveryChanceEventIsSpontaneous EverySpontaneousEventIsChance : Prop) : Prop :=
  EveryChanceEventIsSpontaneous ∧ ¬ EverySpontaneousEventIsChance

/--
Practical restriction in Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b1-8: chance belongs among matters of
action and fortune and therefore requires a capacity for choice. Inanimate things, non-rational
animals, and children do not act by chance and have good or bad fortune only by analogy.

Source: Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b1-8 (Bekker pagination).
CTS work: `urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0086.tlg031`.
-/
def ChanceRequiresPracticalChoiceCapacityReading
    (ChanceConcernsPracticalAction
      ChanceActionRequiresChoice
      InanimateThingsAnimalsChildrenHaveChoice
      NonChoosersActByChance
      FortuneAppliesToNonChoosersWithoutAnalogy : Prop) : Prop :=
  ChanceConcernsPracticalAction ∧
    ChanceActionRequiresChoice ∧
      ¬ InanimateThingsAnimalsChildrenHaveChoice ∧
        ¬ NonChoosersActByChance ∧
          ¬ FortuneAppliesToNonChoosersWithoutAnalogy

/--
Passive qualification in Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b8-12: beings without choice can in
a qualified way be affected by chance when an acting agent does something concerning them by
chance, but not otherwise.

Source: Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b8-12 (Bekker pagination).
CTS work: `urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0086.tlg031`.
-/
def NonChoosersPassivelyAffectedByChanceReading
    (AgentActsByChanceConcerningNonChooser
      NonChooserPassivelyAffectedByChance : Prop) : Prop :=
  AgentActsByChanceConcerningNonChooser ↔ NonChooserPassivelyAffectedByChance

/--
Broader spontaneous scope in Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b12-18: spontaneity applies to
other animals and many inanimate things, illustrated by the horse that arrives and is saved
without coming in order to be saved and the tripod that falls upright without falling in
order to serve as a seat.

Source: Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b12-18 (Bekker pagination).
CTS work: `urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0086.tlg031`.
-/
def SpontaneityExtendsToAnimalsAndInanimateReading
    (SpontaneityAppliesToOtherAnimals
      SpontaneityAppliesToManyInanimateThings
      HorseArrivesAndIsSavedNotForSafety
      TripodFallsUprightNotForSeating : Prop) : Prop :=
  SpontaneityAppliesToOtherAnimals ∧
    SpontaneityAppliesToManyInanimateThings ∧
      HorseArrivesAndIsSavedNotForSafety ∧
        TripodFallsUprightNotForSeating

/--
Definitions in Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b18-23: an externally caused event in the
purposive domain is spontaneous when it is not produced for the outcome that occurs; among
such spontaneous events, those involving objects of choice for agents capable of choice are
from chance.

Source: Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b18-23 (Bekker pagination).
CTS work: `urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0086.tlg031`.
-/
def SpontaneousAndChanceScopeDefinitionReading
    (EventIsPurposive EventProducedForOutcome EventHasExternalCause
      EventIsSpontaneous EventConcernsChoice AgentHasChoice EventIsChance : Prop) : Prop :=
  (EventIsPurposive ∧ ¬ EventProducedForOutcome ∧ EventHasExternalCause →
      EventIsSpontaneous) ∧
    (EventIsSpontaneous ∧ EventConcernsChoice ∧ AgentHasChoice → EventIsChance)

/--
The “in vain” comparison in Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b23-32: an activity is vain when
it is naturally for an end but fails to complete that end; failure to produce an unrelated
outcome is not vain. Aristotle uses this purposive mismatch to illuminate spontaneity.

Source: Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b23-32 (Bekker pagination).
CTS work: `urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0086.tlg031`.
-/
def InVainPurposiveMismatchReading
    (ActivityNaturallyForEnd
      ActivityFailsToCompleteEnd
      ActivityIsVain
      FailureOfUnrelatedOutcomeIsVain
      PurposiveMismatchIlluminatesSpontaneity : Prop) : Prop :=
  (ActivityNaturallyForEnd ∧ ActivityFailsToCompleteEnd → ActivityIsVain) ∧
    ¬ FailureOfUnrelatedOutcomeIsVain ∧
      PurposiveMismatchIlluminatesSpontaneity

/--
Natural-domain distinction in Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b32-37: when something contrary
to nature occurs among things generated by nature, it is called spontaneous rather than from
chance.

Source: Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 197b32-37 (Bekker pagination).
CTS work: `urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0086.tlg031`.
-/
def ContraryToNatureCalledSpontaneousNotChanceReading
    (OutcomeOccursAmongNaturalGenerations
      OutcomeIsContraryToNature
      OutcomeIsSpontaneous
      OutcomeIsChance : Prop) : Prop :=
  OutcomeOccursAmongNaturalGenerations ∧ OutcomeIsContraryToNature →
    OutcomeIsSpontaneous ∧ ¬ OutcomeIsChance

/--
Causal classification in Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 198a1-5: chance and spontaneity belong
to the source-of-motion mode of cause. They are incidental causes of outcomes that nature or
thought could properly cause, and the possible incidental causes are indefinite in number.

Source: Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 198a1-5 (Bekker pagination).
CTS work: `urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0086.tlg031`.
-/
def ChanceSpontaneityEfficientIncidentalCausesReading
    (ChanceSpontaneityBelongToSourceOfMotionMode
      ChanceSpontaneityAreIncidentalCauses
      NatureOrThoughtCouldProperlyCauseTheirOutcomes
      TheirIncidentalCausesAreIndefinite : Prop) : Prop :=
  ChanceSpontaneityBelongToSourceOfMotionMode ∧
    ChanceSpontaneityAreIncidentalCauses ∧
      NatureOrThoughtCouldProperlyCauseTheirOutcomes ∧
        TheirIncidentalCausesAreIndefinite

/--
Priority bridge in Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 198a5-13: because no incidental cause is prior
to its proper cause, if chance and spontaneity are incidental while mind or nature supplies
the corresponding proper cause, chance and spontaneity are posterior to mind and nature.
This remains so even under the supposition that spontaneity has the greatest possible role
in causing the heavens.

Source: Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 198a5-13 (Bekker pagination).
CTS work: `urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0086.tlg031`.
-/
def IncidentalPriorityImpliesChancePosteriorityReading
    (ChanceSpontaneityAreIncidentalCauses
      NatureOrThoughtCouldProperlyCauseTheirOutcomes
      NoIncidentalCausePriorToProperCause
      ChanceSpontaneityPosteriorToMindAndNature
      CosmicSpontaneityWouldNotDisplacePriorMindNature : Prop) : Prop :=
  NoIncidentalCausePriorToProperCause ∧
    (ChanceSpontaneityAreIncidentalCauses ∧
        NatureOrThoughtCouldProperlyCauseTheirOutcomes →
      ChanceSpontaneityPosteriorToMindAndNature ∧
        CosmicSpontaneityWouldNotDisplacePriorMindNature)

/--
Conclusion in Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 198a9-13: chance and spontaneity are posterior to
mind and nature. Even if spontaneity were granted the greatest possible role in causing the
heavens, mind and nature would still be prior causes of this whole and many other things.

Source: Aristotle, *Physics* II.6, 198a9-13 (Bekker pagination).
CTS work: `urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0086.tlg031`.
-/
def ChanceSpontaneityPosteriorMindNatureReading
    (ChanceSpontaneityPosteriorToMindAndNature
      CosmicSpontaneityWouldNotDisplacePriorMindNature : Prop) : Prop :=
  ChanceSpontaneityPosteriorToMindAndNature ∧
    CosmicSpontaneityWouldNotDisplacePriorMindNature

end TruthCore.Greek.Aristotle.Physics
